      <h2>The Great Void in Indian Asset Management</h2>
      <p>
        For decades, Indian investors faced a stark, binary divide when allocating capital into professional wealth management:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Retail Mutual Funds:</strong> Highly accessible (starting at ₹100 or ₹500 via SIP), but tightly constrained by SEBI regulations that prohibit naked short-selling, unhedged derivatives, or flexible asymmetric risk management.</li>
        <li><strong>Portfolio Management Services (PMS):</strong> Required a minimum ticket of <strong>₹50 Lakhs</strong>, featuring complex individualized tax accounting and significant fee loads.</li>
        <li><strong>Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs):</strong> Required a minimum commitment of <strong>₹1 Crore</strong>, locking capital into multi-year private equity or hedge fund structures.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This left hundreds of thousands of affluent Indian retail investors, emerging High-Net-Worth Individuals (HNIs), and tech professionals stranded. You had ₹10 Lakhs to ₹30 Lakhs to deploy, desired sophisticated hedging strategies that don't plummet during market downturns, but were locked out of PMS and AIFs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Enter SIF (Specialized Investment Funds).</strong> Regulated by SEBI and indexed by AMFI, SIF represents the most consequential financial product innovation in India in over a decade.
      </p>

