        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Taxation of SIF in India: What Investors Must Know</h2>
      <p>
        One of the biggest advantages of SIF over PMS is <strong>tax efficiency at the fund level</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>In a PMS:</strong> Every time the portfolio manager sells a stock or executes a hedging trade, an actionable taxable event occurs in your individual Demat account. High-churn PMS portfolios can generate dozens of pages of short-term capital gains tax liabilities.</li>
        <li><strong>In a SIF:</strong> Because the fund is structured as a mutual fund trust, internal portfolio rebalancing and derivative trades do <em>not</em> trigger immediate capital gains tax for you. You are only taxed when you redeem units of the SIF scheme.</li>
        <li><strong>Redemption Tax Rates:</strong>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Equity-oriented SIFs (65%+ domestic equity):</strong> Long-term capital gains (LTCG) above ₹1.25 Lakh are taxed at 12.5% (holding period &gt; 12 months). Short-term capital gains (STCG) are taxed at 20%.</li>
            <li><strong>Debt or Non-Equity SIFs:</strong> Taxed at your applicable income tax slab rate.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

